WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.167
Zero Trust considers the device involved in an access request because a valid identity using a poorly protected endpoint may still create significant risk.

2
00:00:10.167 --> 00:00:14.099
Identity confidence and device confidence answer different questions.

3
00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:23.298
Device posture can include whether the endpoint is known, managed, supported, properly configured, encrypted, and reporting current security information.

4
00:00:23.298 --> 00:00:29.045
The exact signals depend on organizational policy, technology, and resource sensitivity.

5
00:00:29.045 --> 00:00:32.138
A registered device is not automatically safe forever.

6
00:00:32.138 --> 00:00:39.861
Configuration can drift, security tools can stop reporting, software can become outdated, and ownership can change.

7
00:00:39.861 --> 00:00:43.403
Current posture is more useful than a one-time enrollment record.

8
00:00:43.403 --> 00:00:50.997
Organizations may distinguish among corporate-managed, personally owned, partner-managed, shared, and specialized devices.

9
00:00:50.997 --> 00:00:57.292
Each category can have different levels of assurance and may receive different access to sensitive resources.

10
00:00:57.292 --> 00:00:59.627
Access decisions should be proportionate.

11
00:00:59.627 --> 00:01:10.343
A low-risk informational service may tolerate a broader range of devices, while administrative functions or sensitive data may require a managed endpoint with stronger posture evidence.

12
00:01:10.343 --> 00:01:16.580
When a device does not meet policy, the response does not always need to be a simple permanent denial.

13
00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:27.555
Depending on risk and business need, the organization may limit access, require another verification step, direct remediation, or provide an approved alternative.

14
00:01:27.555 --> 00:01:31.317
Endpoint visibility supports monitoring after access is granted.

15
00:01:31.317 --> 00:01:42.661
Changes in device health, unusual activity, or loss of management coverage can trigger reevaluation and help security teams understand whether continued access remains appropriate.

16
00:01:42.661 --> 00:01:48.309
Device evaluation must also respect privacy, legal, and workforce requirements.

17
00:01:48.309 --> 00:01:54.914
Governance should define which signals are collected, why they are needed, how they are protected, and who may use them.
